      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Monroe County was carved out of Arkansas and Phillips counties in 1829 and named in honor of President James Monroe.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/monroe_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first permanent settlement in the area appeared in 1810 when John A. Montague built a homestead on the White River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/monroe_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Between 1824 and 1828, pioneers used military roads to clear bald cypress and oak forests, turning the Delta into cotton fields.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/monroe_arkansas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the 20th century, Monroe stayed a small rural community whose economy centered on cotton, rice, soybeans, and corn.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/monroe_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The 1927 Great Flood devastated the Delta, but New Deal projects like the WPA helped rebuild flood control and infrastructure.",
